<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abkhazia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Abkhazia, Apsny</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abkhazia, officially the Republic of Abkhazia, is a partially recognised state on the eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a>, which most countries recognise as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Within the Georgian Soviet Socialist Republic it was the Abkhaz Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic. Ethnic tensions between Abkhaz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> disintegrated led to the 1992–1993 War in Abkhazia, after which Georgia lost control of most of the territory and Georgians were expelled in what is described as ethnic cleansing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, Venezuela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> recognise it; Georgia maintains a government-in-exile.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Abkhazia covers about 8,665 square kilometres at the western end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. The Caucasus Mountains to the north and north-east separate it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the Georgian region of Samegrelo-Zemo Svaneti lies to the east and south-east, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the south and south-west. Its capital and largest city is Sukhumi. The Abkhaz name for the land is Apsny, etymologised as 'a land of the soul', although the literal meaning is 'a country of mortals'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Between the 9th and 6th centuries BCE the territory was part of the ancient Kingdom of Colchis, and around the 6th century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> founded trading colonies at Pitiunt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dioscurias">Dioscurias</a>. The Kingdom of Abkhazia flourished between 850 and 950 CE and ended in unification with the eastern Georgian states under King Bagrat III. On 2 July 1810 Russian Marines stormed Sukhum-Kale, and Abkhazia joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> as an autonomous principality.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1921, Abkhazia became a Soviet republic associated with Georgia, and in 1931 an autonomous republic within it. In June 1988 a manifesto known as the Abkhazian Letter was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>, and on 16 July 1989 violence broke out in Sukhumi.</p><p>In August 1992 the National Guard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> entered Abkhazia, and the War in Abkhazia began. Georgia lost control of most of the territory. Before the war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> made up nearly half of the population; about 5,000 people were killed and up to 250,000 Georgians were expelled, in what is described as ethnic cleansing by Abkhaz separatists. A ceasefire was agreed in 1994. In August 2008 Abkhaz and Russian forces fought Georgian forces, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> recognised Abkhazia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Abkhazia is a presidential republic with a People's Assembly of 35 members. Its 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 240,705 inhabitants, of whom 50.7 per cent were Abkhaz. Abkhaz is the official language and Russian a language of state. Four UN member states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, Venezuela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, recognise it as independent. Most countries regard it as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, which maintains a government in exile.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abkhazia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
